  <name><text>Ch10_P04_Ex1_Find_the_error</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof from the exercise above, in numbered lines. The statement is true: \(7^n + 1\) is divisible by 8 for every odd positive integer \(n\). But the proof below does not prove it.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>When \(n = 1\), \(f(1) = 7^1 + 1 = 8\), which is divisible by 8, so the statement holds for \(n = 1\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Assume that the statement holds for \(n = k\), so that \(7^k + 1 = 8m\) for some positive integer \(m\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>We need to show that the statement holds for \(n = k + 1\), that is, that \(7^{k+1} + 1 = 8l\) for some positive integer \(l\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\(7^{k+1} + 1 = 7 \times 7^k + 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>\(\phantom{7^{k+1} + 1} = 7 \times 7^k + 7 - 6\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(\phantom{7^{k+1} + 1} = 7(7^k + 1) - 6\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>\(\phantom{7^{k+1} + 1} = 7 \times 8m - 6\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>\(\phantom{7^{k+1} + 1} = 8(7m) - 6\), which is not a multiple of 8.</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Which line contains the error?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>What is wrong with it? (Optional: this box is not marked and not saved.)</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div></li>
<li><p>For which \(n\) from 1 to 6 is \(7^n + 1\) divisible by 8? Enter the set of these values of \(n\) in curly brackets, for example <code>{1, 2}</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Check the proof against what an induction must do: a base case, and a step from each case covered by the statement to the next one. The base case (line 1) is correct, and so is every line of algebra (lines 4 to 8): each follows from the one before it.</p>
<p>The error is the target in line 3. The statement is only about odd \(n\), so the hypothesis in line 2 is about an odd number \(k\), and the next case is the next odd number, \(k + 2\). Line 3 aims instead at \(k + 1\), which is even, and the statement says nothing about even numbers.</p>
<p>In fact line 8 is true: \(8(7m) - 6\) is not a multiple of 8. It leaves remainder 2 on division by 8, so for even \(n\) the number \(7^n + 1\) is never a multiple of 8. The values in part (c) confirm this:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>\(n\)</th><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><th>\(7^n + 1\)</th><td>8</td><td>50</td><td>344</td><td>2402</td><td>16808</td><td>117650</td></tr>
<tr><th>remainder on division by 8</th><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So \(7^n + 1\) is divisible by 8 exactly for \(n = 1, 3, 5\), and no proof could ever pass from \(k\) to \(k + 1\). The next check repairs the proof.</p>
<p>A reason you might have written: <q>Line 3: if \(k\) is odd, then \(k + 1\) is even, and the statement says nothing about even numbers; the step should go to \(k + 2\).</q></p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
